  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A New York paper reported bat-winged people living on the moon. The Sun ran it as news in August eighteen thirty-five.",
        "visual_query": "1830s printing press newsroom",
        "visual_fallback": "old newspaper"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Six articles. Lunar bison, goats with a single horn, beaches, temples, and Vespertilio-homo, man-bats who walked upright and flew.",
        "visual_query": "full moon night telescope",
        "visual_fallback": "moon closeup"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was all credited to Sir John Herschel, a real astronomer, genuinely observing at the Cape of Good Hope. He knew nothing about it.",
        "visual_query": "victorian observatory brass telescope",
        "visual_fallback": "antique telescope"
      },
      {
        "narration": "New York believed it. A committee of Yale scientists came down to read the original journal articles. There were none. Who writes this, and why?",
        "visual_query": "nineteenth century new york street",
        "visual_fallback": "crowded old street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Richard Adams Locke, a Sun reporter, wrote it as satire of serious claims that the moon was populated. The Sun admitted the fabrication on the sixteenth of September and never printed a retraction.",
        "visual_query": "antique metal printing type",
        "visual_fallback": "printing type"
      }
    ],
